#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:311
msgid "Why is `Buffer` unsafe?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:313
msgid ""
"Today, the node.js `Buffer` constructor is overloaded to handle many "
"different argument types like `String`, `Array`, `Object`, "
"`TypedArrayView` (`Uint8Array`, etc.), `ArrayBuffer`, and also `Number`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:317
msgid ""
"The API is optimized for convenience: you can throw any type at it, and "
"it will try to do what you want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:320
msgid ""
"Because the Buffer constructor is so powerful, you often see code like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:329
msgid "***But what happens if `toHex` is called with a `Number` argument?***"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:331
msgid "Remote Memory Disclosure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:333
msgid ""
"If an attacker can make your program call the `Buffer` constructor with a"
" `Number` argument, then they can make it allocate uninitialized memory "
"from the node.js process. This could potentially disclose TLS private "
"keys, user data, or database passwords."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:337
msgid ""
"When the `Buffer` constructor is passed a `Number` argument, it returns "
"an **UNINITIALIZED** block of memory of the specified `size`. When you "
"create a `Buffer` like this, you **MUST** overwrite the contents before "
"returning it to the user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:341
msgid ""
"From the [node.js "
"docs](https://nodejs.org/api/buffer.html#buffer_new_buffer_size):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:343
msgid "`new Buffer(size)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:345
msgid "`size` Number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:347
msgid ""
"The underlying memory for `Buffer` instances created in this way is not "
"initialized. **The contents of a newly created `Buffer` are unknown and "
"could contain sensitive data.** Use `buf.fill(0)` to initialize a Buffer "
"to zeroes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:351
msgid "(Emphasis our own.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:353
msgid ""
"Whenever the programmer intended to create an uninitialized `Buffer` you "
"often see code like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:366
msgid "Would this ever be a problem in real code?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:368
msgid ""
"Yes. It's surprisingly common to forget to check the type of your "
"variables in a dynamically-typed language like JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:371
msgid ""
"Usually the consequences of assuming the wrong type is that your program "
"crashes with an uncaught exception. But the failure mode for forgetting "
"to check the type of arguments to the `Buffer` constructor is more "
"catastrophic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:375
msgid ""
"Here's an example of a vulnerable service that takes a JSON payload and "
"converts it to hex:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:395
msgid "In this example, an http client just has to send:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:403
msgid "and it will get back 1,000 bytes of uninitialized memory from the server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:405
msgid ""
"This is a very serious bug. It's similar in severity to the [the "
"Heartbleed bug](http://heartbleed.com/) that allowed disclosure of "
"OpenSSL process memory by remote attackers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:410
msgid "Which real-world packages were vulnerable?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:412
msgid "[`bittorrent-dht`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/bittorrent-dht)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:414
msgid ""
"[Mathias Buus](https://github.com/mafintosh) and I ([Feross "
"Aboukhadijeh](http://feross.org/)) found this issue in one of our own "
"packages, [`bittorrent-dht`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/bittorrent-"
"dht). The bug would allow anyone on the internet to send a series of "
"messages to a user of `bittorrent-dht` and get them to reveal 20 bytes at"
" a time of uninitialized memory from the node.js process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:420
msgid ""
"Here's [the commit](https://github.com/feross/bittorrent-"
"dht/commit/6c7da04025d5633699800a99ec3fbadf70ad35b8) that fixed it. We "
"released a new fixed version, created a [Node Security Project "
"disclosure](https://nodesecurity.io/advisories/68), and deprecated all "
"vulnerable versions on npm so users will get a warning to upgrade to a "
"newer version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:426
msgid "[`ws`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ws)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:428
msgid ""
"That got us wondering if there were other vulnerable packages. Sure "
"enough, within a short period of time, we found the same issue in "
"[`ws`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ws), the most popular WebSocket "
"implementation in node.js."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:432
msgid ""
"If certain APIs were called with `Number` parameters instead of `String` "
"or `Buffer` as expected, then uninitialized server memory would be "
"disclosed to the remote peer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:435
msgid "These were the vulnerable methods:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:443
msgid "Here's a vulnerable socket server with some echo functionality:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:456
msgid "`socket.send(number)` called on the server, will disclose server memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:458
msgid ""
"Here's [the release](https://github.com/websockets/ws/releases/tag/1.0.1)"
" where the issue was fixed, with a more detailed explanation. Props to "
"[Arnout Kazemier](https://github.com/3rd-Eden) for the quick fix. Here's "
"the [Node Security Project "
"disclosure](https://nodesecurity.io/advisories/67)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:464
msgid "What's the solution?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:466
msgid ""
"It's important that node.js offers a fast way to get memory otherwise "
"performance-critical applications would needlessly get a lot slower."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:469
msgid ""
"But we need a better way to *signal our intent* as programmers. **When we"
" want uninitialized memory, we should request it explicitly.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:472
msgid ""
"Sensitive functionality should not be packed into a developer-friendly "
"API that loosely accepts many different types. This type of API "
"encourages the lazy practice of passing variables in without checking the"
" type very carefully."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:476
msgid "A new API: `Buffer.allocUnsafe(number)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:478
msgid ""
"The functionality of creating buffers with uninitialized memory should be"
" part of another API. We propose `Buffer.allocUnsafe(number)`. This way, "
"it's not part of an API that frequently gets user input of all sorts of "
"different types passed into it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:492
msgid "How do we fix node.js core?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:494
msgid ""
"We sent [a PR to node.js core](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/4514) "
"(merged as `semver-major`) which defends against one case:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:502
msgid ""
"In this situation, it's implied that the programmer intended the first "
"argument to be a string, since they passed an encoding as a second "
"argument. Today, node.js will allocate uninitialized memory in the case "
"of `new Buffer(number, encoding)`, which is probably not what the "
"programmer intended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:507
msgid ""
"But this is only a partial solution, since if the programmer does `new "
"Buffer(variable)` (without an `encoding` parameter) there's no way to "
"know what they intended. If `variable` is sometimes a number, then "
"uninitialized memory will sometimes be returned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:511
msgid "What's the real long-term fix?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:513
msgid ""
"We could deprecate and remove `new Buffer(number)` and use "
"`Buffer.allocUnsafe(number)` when we need uninitialized memory. But that "
"would break 1000s of packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:516
msgid "~~We believe the best solution is to:~~"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:518
msgid "~~1. Change `new Buffer(number)` to return safe, zeroed-out memory~~"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:520
msgid ""
"~~2. Create a new API for creating uninitialized Buffers. We propose: "
"`Buffer.allocUnsafe(number)`~~"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:522
msgid "Update"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:524
msgid "We now support adding three new APIs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:526
msgid "`Buffer.from(value)` - convert from any type to a buffer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:527
msgid "`Buffer.alloc(size)` - create a zero-filled buffer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:528
msgid ""
"`Buffer.allocUnsafe(size)` - create an uninitialized buffer with given "
"size"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:530
msgid ""
"This solves the core problem that affected `ws` and `bittorrent-dht` "
"which is `Buffer(variable)` getting tricked into taking a number "
"argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:533
msgid ""
"This way, existing code continues working and the impact on the npm "
"ecosystem will be minimal. Over time, npm maintainers can migrate "
"performance-critical code to use `Buffer.allocUnsafe(number)` instead of "
"`new Buffer(number)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:538
msgid "Conclusion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:540
msgid ""
"We think there's a serious design issue with the `Buffer` API as it "
"exists today. It promotes insecure software by putting high-risk "
"functionality into a convenient API with friendly \"developer "
"ergonomics\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:544
msgid ""
"This wasn't merely a theoretical exercise because we found the issue in "
"some of the most popular npm packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:547
msgid ""
"Fortunately, there's an easy fix that can be applied today. Use `safe-"
"buffer` in place of `buffer`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safe-buffer_README.md:554
msgid ""
"Eventually, we hope that node.js core can switch to this new, safer "
"behavior. We believe the impact on the ecosystem would be minimal since "
"it's not a breaking change. Well-maintained, popular packages would be "
"updated to use `Buffer.alloc` quickly, while older, insecure packages "
"would magically become safe from this attack vector."
msgstr ""

